WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:07.440
So let's say that you have joined the team and you got an access to a database.

00:07.440 --> 00:12.560
The first thing that I usually do is that I explore the structure of the database, just to have basic

00:12.560 --> 00:16.800
understandings about the database tables, the views columns.

00:16.840 --> 00:20.200
Are we talking about like tables, hundreds of tables.

00:20.200 --> 00:23.680
So it is just a few queries in order to say hello to the database.

00:23.680 --> 00:27.480
So now let's go to SQL and explore the database of our projects.

00:27.480 --> 00:28.560
So now how are we going to do it.

00:28.560 --> 00:33.480
Either you go to the left side over here and start clicking the objects of your database and explore

00:33.480 --> 00:35.480
the tables, views, columns and so on.

00:35.480 --> 00:39.880
Or a better way that I usually do is that I explore the database using a query.

00:39.880 --> 00:46.720
So what we can do, we can go and select data from the system tables, because the database stores metadata

00:46.720 --> 00:49.360
informations about our tables and objects.

00:49.360 --> 00:52.160
So we're going to target an information schema.

00:52.360 --> 00:58.200
This is an internal schema in the database where we have like multiple tables and views to explore the

00:58.200 --> 01:00.600
metadata and the structure of our database.

01:00.600 --> 01:03.280
So for example we can go with tables.

01:03.280 --> 01:05.560
So let's go and query it.

01:05.560 --> 01:08.120
And with that you have a list of tables.

01:08.120 --> 01:13.760
And with that you can see multiple informations like a catalog the schema and the table names.

01:13.760 --> 01:17.440
And you can see over here the object type whether it is a table or a view.

01:17.440 --> 01:22.080
If you done the data warehouse project with me then you will find a lot of tables.

01:22.080 --> 01:27.040
But if you are just doing the data analysis you will see only the three tables.

01:27.040 --> 01:29.640
So customers, products and sales.

01:29.640 --> 01:34.840
So with that we can see in our database there are like around 15 tables or three tables.

01:34.880 --> 01:40.040
Now in the output you can see the database name, the schema and a list of all tables.

01:40.080 --> 01:44.560
And of course don't forget that you are using the database that we created.

01:44.560 --> 01:48.440
So with that we have a nice quick list with all tables inside our database.

01:48.440 --> 01:54.120
Now the next step we can go and drill down and check what are the columns that we have inside our database.

01:54.120 --> 01:57.040
And for that we can as well target the same schema.

01:57.040 --> 01:59.960
So select star from information schema.

01:59.960 --> 02:01.200
And it is very simple.

02:01.200 --> 02:03.400
So we're going to go to the table columns.

02:03.400 --> 02:05.440
So let's go and execute it.

02:05.440 --> 02:08.560
And now we will see a lot of informations over here.

02:08.560 --> 02:15.530
So we can see in our database we have around 101 columns so that we can see all the columns available

02:15.530 --> 02:16.410
in our database.

02:16.410 --> 02:22.730
And what I usually do is that I go and select the columns only for specific table, so we can say where

02:22.730 --> 02:24.450
table name equal.

02:25.970 --> 02:29.010
Let's get for example the dimension customers.

02:29.010 --> 02:31.050
So let's query the whole thing.

02:31.050 --> 02:34.610
And with that we can see we have ten columns inside this dimension.

02:34.610 --> 02:38.450
And this is how the columns are sorted inside our table or view.

02:38.450 --> 02:42.850
And we can see all the metadata informations about each column.

02:42.850 --> 02:46.730
So now as you can see we are now exploring the structure of our database.

02:46.730 --> 02:50.810
And this is really helpful to get an overview of the database and the projects.

02:50.850 --> 02:54.530
Are we talking about like 20 tables or hundreds of tables.

02:54.530 --> 02:58.410
And we can quickly see the naming of the columns, the tables.

02:58.410 --> 03:04.450
This is really important to get a feeling about the projects and sets the foundations for exploring

03:04.450 --> 03:06.450
the data inside those tables.

03:06.490 --> 03:08.490
All right friends, so with that, we have done the first step.

03:08.490 --> 03:13.370
We have explored the database structure and now we can start diving into the actual data.

03:13.410 --> 03:16.530
The first thing that we can explore is the dimensions.
